00:00:02.149 --> 00:00:05.030 align:start position:0%
precision medicine is changing the way  we understand diagnose and treat cancer  the transformation is driven by high  throughput molecular data from patients  animal models and large-scale cell line  experiments  in this program we will explore how such  high throughput data can be analyzed  to study the genomic background of  cancer we will see  how to study omics data that describes  the basic biology associated with cancer  onset  development and outcomes and we'll see  how large-scale data sets collected from  clinical trials provide an opportunity  to better understand this condition  and improve the precision of clinical  oncology  we will break down the complexities of  tumor development  and the role of tumor microenvironment  we will also speak about  the dna genes and rna transcription  and see how these elements are studied  using next generation sequencing data  we will learn about the basic and  translational research in oncology  where patient-derived cell lines  xenograft models  and clinical trials offer a rich  collection of data to inform diagnosis  and treatment of cancer in patients  the recent growth in high throughput  sequencing of cancer genomes and  transcriptomes has produced  a big data problem that precludes many  cancer biologists and oncologists from  gleaning knowledge from these data  regarding the nature of malignant  processes and  identifying the relationship between  tumor genomic profiles  and treatment response that's why we  will learn about these data types  find them on repositories like the ncbi  and see how to efficiently process and  analyze such data  together we will map visualize and  detect significant changes in dna  sequences  using a reference genome and paired  tumor and normal samples  we will use rna-seq analysis to run a  full processing pipeline  and study differentially expressed genes  using  such methods like dec 2 and edge r  this will allow us to find relationships  in the data  and identify significant genes that are  associated with cancer types  or stages we will learn about gene set  enrichment analysis  pathways activated or deactivated in  various tumors  and we will also speak about pathway  deregulation and the effect that many  subtle changes can have  on this condition these pathways will  give us insight  into the biology of tumor development  and how it interacts  with the microenvironment we will also  learn about the cancer genome atlas  finding data learning how to navigate  the genome data commons portal  and download multi-omics datasets with  clinical information about  samples we will also look at cancer  studies using patient-derived cell lines  where effective treatment can be derived  by looking at  profiles at the molecular basis we'll  also speak about the role big data plays  in biomedical research  as well as its clinical applications  where data from biopsies  is used to diagnose patients into novel  types of cancer  we will take samples from the liver  heptacellular carcinoma tcga project  and see how various clinical data  available for those patients can be used  in the analysis of gene and microrna  expression  the program will leverage our top-rated  online tutorials with smart tracking of  activity and curated data sets designed  for a great learning experience  you will also learn about methods of  dealing with data  in r and python and leverage our big  data analysis tools  where you can analyze huge omics data  sets without any technical background  in bioinformatics and without any  limitations of your personal  infrastructure  at the end of the program participants  will receive a certificate of  participation  in the previous programs many of the  participants completed the coursework  and continued on to leverage the  information they gained from the course  in their own research and clinical  applications  precision medicine is changing the way  we understand  diagnose and treat cancer we welcome you  to join this program  and learn about the bioinformatics  component for precision oncology
 

